::ENG4_U05_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which revision fixes the comma splice? Original: The report used current data, it did not explain its method.
{
~A. The report used current data it did not explain its method.#Incorrect. This creates a run-on sentence and does not fix the boundary error.
=B. The report used current data, but it did not explain its method.#Correct. The comma and coordinating conjunction correctly join two complete contrasting ideas.
~C. Because the report used current data.#Incorrect. This is a fragment because it does not complete the thought.
~D. The report, used current data, it did not explain.#Incorrect. The punctuation interrupts the sentence and leaves a comma splice.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source: Concise updates helped teams identify delays earlier. Which paraphrase preserves meaning?
{
=A. Brief status updates helped teams notice delays sooner.#Correct. This accurately restates the source in new wording.
~B. Long updates caused all delays.#Incorrect. This changes the source meaning.
~C. Teams never identified delays.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~D. Updates were unrelated to delays.#Incorrect. This removes the source relationship.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Which sentence uses academic vocabulary precisely?
{
~A. The convention is good because grammar stuff is good.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. The sentence is credible because it is long.#Incorrect. Length does not create credibility.
=C. The revision improves precision by clarifying the relationship between evidence and reasoning.#Correct. This uses academic vocabulary accurately.
~D. The punctuation is random and therefore professional.#Incorrect. Professional writing requires purposeful punctuation.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision best strengthens clarity? Original: The evidence is relevant; because it supports the claim.
{
~A. The evidence is relevant; because, it supports the claim.#Incorrect. This still misuses the semicolon and adds unnecessary punctuation.
~B. The evidence is relevant, because it supports the claim, and.#Incorrect. This creates an awkward and incomplete ending.
~C. The evidence, relevant because it supports the claim.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
=D. The evidence is relevant because it supports the claim.#Correct. The dependent clause belongs with the main clause without a semicolon.
}
::ENG4_U05_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence correctly uses a colon?
{
~A. The paragraph needs: because the evidence is unclear.#Incorrect. A colon should not separate a verb from its needed complement this way.
=B. The paragraph needs one major revision: the evidence must be explained.#Correct. The colon introduces the specific revision needed.
~C. The paragraph: needs one major revision the evidence.#Incorrect. This breaks the sentence structure.
~D. Because: the paragraph needs evidence.#Incorrect. This misuses the colon after a subordinating conjunction.
}
